      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 SEICAP. Published by Elsevier Espana. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The pathogenesis of exercise-induced bronchoconstriction (EIB) in asthma is incompletely understood. The role of exhaled breath condensate (EBC) annexin A5, which is an anti-inflammatory mediator, has not been investigated. The purpose of this study is to evaluate EBC annexin A5 levels in EIB in asthmatic children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Two groups of children were enrolled in this study: asthmatic children with positive (n=11) and negative (n=7) responses to exercise. The levels of pre- and post-exercise EBC annexin A5 were determined with using enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "We observed significant higher pre-exercise EBC annexin A5 levels in the challenge test negative children than in the challenge test positive children (p<0.05). No significant difference was observed in the post-exercise EBC annexin A5 levels between the groups (p>0.05). Also, no significant difference was observed between pre- and post-exercise EBC annexin A5 levels within each group (p>0.05). There was an inverse correlation between annexin A5 levels and a reduction in forced expiratory volume at one second percent (FEV1%) (p=0.009, r=-0.598)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Our preliminary study showed that EBC annexin A5 may have a possible preventive role in EIB in asthma. Annexin A5 and related compounds may provide novel therapeutic approaches to the treatment of EIB in asthma."
          }
        ]
      },
